I bet you're confused when it comes to  the different types of cooking oils  which is healthy which should you avoid  what are the health benefits and how to  use it to make things a little less  confusing I'm gonna share with you some  of the facts about the top 10 most  common cooking oils all cooking oils are  composed of three different types of  fatty acids monounsaturated  polyunsaturated and saturated fats so  each oil is categorized based on which  fatty acid is most prominent in it so  before we get started let's understand  the difference between monounsaturated  polyunsaturated and also saturated fats  mono unsaturated fats are typically  liquid at room temperature but may  solidify when chilled it can help to  reduce bad cholesterol levels and also  it is high in vitamin E so olive oil and  avocado oil are considered mostly mono  unsaturated fats there are two main  types of polyunsaturated fats omega-3  fatty acids and also omega-6 fatty acids  which are both essential to maintain a  healthy body  so grapeseed oil is high in  polyunsaturated fats saturated fats are  typically found in animal products such  as meat or dairy but they are also found  in some plant sources such as coconut  oil these fats are typically solid at  room temperature so let's start with  olive oil regular olive oil has an  unremarkable smell and a neutral palate  it has a high smoke point at 465  Fahrenheit making it one of the most  versatile cooking ones it is great for  sauteing pan frying grilling deep frying  and even great as a salad dressing  olive oil is known for its heart-healthy  benefits it is high in vitamin E and  also monounsaturated fats  however it is very important to note  that extra virgin olive oil should not  be used for cooking extra virgin olive  oil is processed by Co pressing with  temperatures no higher than 80 point 5  Fahrenheit which means it is richer in  antioxidants nutrients and also flavors  has a slight fruity bitter II and also  peppery taste and always remember to  keep your olive oil in a cold dark and  dry place to avoid it from going rancid  the next oil is coconut oil coconut oil  is nearly colorless and has a very light  coconut taste and aroma which you will  taste in your cooking coconut oil has  the highest amount of saturated fats  with more than 90% what this also means  is that it is very resistant to heat  making it extremely versatile it is  great for frying sauteing and great as a  butter replacement in baked recipes  especially for vegans so what are the  benefits coconut oil is high in vitamin  E K iron an especially high in a fatty  acid called lauric acid which can  actually help to raise your good  cholesterol and kill harmful bacteria  viruses and fungi and the fatty acid is  also believed to help reduce hunger now  let's move on to flaxseed oil which is  also known as linseed oil it has a clear  golden yellow color and has a strong  nutty and bitter taste which is  certainly not everybody's favorite you  shouldn't be cooking with flaxseed oil  use it in small quantity as a salad  dressing or add into smoothies because  too much can have a very strong taste  and also aroma it contains high amounts  of omega-3 fatty acids especially our  FAL in oleic acid which is important for  proper functioning of the brain and also  hot consuming it on a daily basis can  also help treat constipation and improve  on your overall digestive system the  food oil is avocado oil the composition  of avocado oil is very similar to olive  oil and can be used just like olive oil  its vibrant green and has a very subtle  buttery flavor with the hint of avocado  it has one of the highest smoke points  of any oils at 510  Fahrenheit making it the best choice for  super high cooking such as grilling of  frying but it can also be drizzled on to  salads almost 70% of avocado oil  consists of heart-healthy oleic acid a  mono unsaturated omega-9 fatty acid it  helps to improve heart health and reduce  bad cholesterol levels it is also high  in lutein and antioxidant that help to  boost eye health and reduce any  age-related eye diseases now let's talk  about grapeseed oil some researchers  would categorize grapeseed as a healthy  oil whereas others would say to avoid it  while grapeseed oil has its benefit and  also it's bad sight let's start with the  good grapeseed oil has a good amount of  vitamin E in fact it has doubled the  amount of vitamin E compared to olive  oil however grapeseed oil has one of the  highest amount of omega-6 in comparison  to other vegetable oils omega-6 isn't  bad by nature however too much of  omega-6 in our diet can cause  inflammation increases cholesterol and  caused hormone imbalance the bottom line  is grapeseed oil is a better alternative  for olive oil compared to other  vegetable oils it is also a cheaper  alternative it is virtually flavorless  and odorless which will not alter the  taste of a recipe but use in small  amounts sesame oil is a favorite oil  used in Asian cooking it has a dark  brown color and a toasted nutty flavor  it has a high smoke point making it an  extremely versatile oil but remember to  keep it in the fridge after using it has  high amounts of nutrients including  magnesium calcium zinc potassium and  folate this helps to improve bone health  and boosts circulation now let's take a  look at some of the worst cooking oils  which you think might be healthy canola  oil canola oil might be high in omega-3  fatty acids and low in saturated fats  but it is genetically modified from  rapeseed oil and it's very cheap to  manufacture  hence a large part have already turned  into trans fat it is a refined oil that  is often partially hydrogenated to  increase its stability but this also  increases its negative health effects  peanut oil is commonly referred to as  ground nut oil it has a very high smoke  point and is commonly used in Asian  cooking the restaurants and fast food  joints love it the issue here is that  most of the commercialized peanut oils  you get in grocery stores are highly  refined durai's and bleached so unless  you're able to sauce for the  cold-pressed unrefined version avoid it  sunflower oil is the most commonly used  cooking oil in the world however it does  not contain a significant amount of  minerals or vitamins  Industrial sunflower oil is highly  processed refined and may even contain  trans fats regular consumption of  sunflower oil can lead to heart diseases  high cholesterol levels and other  serious illnesses you should not be  cooking with sunflower oil in fact you  should just avoid it finally if you come  across the vegetable oil well simply  avoid it again it is highly processed  and may be a blend of many different  oils here are the cooking oils which you  should avoid altogether I'm good to  share with you some of the do's and also  don'ts the first rule of thumb is as  much as you can buy the raw unrefined  version avoid highly processed cooking  oils due to the high levels of toxic  chemicals always use cooking oils in  moderation store them in a cool and dry  area away from direct sunlight some oils  have to be stored in the fridge such as  avocado oil flaxseed oil and a sauce  sesame oil so do read the instructions  so I hope that this video has helped you  to better understand some of the most  common cooking oils which to use wish to  avoid and the right way to use them  don't forget to give this video a thumbs  up share this information on and  subscribe to my youtube channel for a  lot more nutritional tips do check out  more useful videos right  all the best
 

